    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/nature09396",
    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Global hydrology",
    "problem": "The absence of direct, global-scale observational constraints on land evapotranspiration has created significant uncertainty about recent changes in the land water cycle, a key diagnostic for understanding the effects of climate change and variability.",
    "method": "We propose a data-driven method using a machine-learning algorithm to upscale in situ measurements from a global monitoring network with meteorological and remote-sensing data to create a global estimate of land evapotranspiration.",
    "task": "The task is to create a data-driven, global estimate of land evapotranspiration from 1982 to 2008 by combining field measurements, remote sensing, and meteorological data to analyze its long-term trend and identify the drivers of its recent decline.",
    "model": [
        "MTE",
        "GSWP-2"
